[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"SRP: Architectural Impacts, Fat Models, and God Classes"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to the Single Responsibility Principle"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) is the 'S' in the SOLID principles. Originally defined by Robert C. Martin, SRP states that a class should have only one reason to change. In architectural terms, this means a module or class should encapsulate responsibility to a single actor or stakeholder."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"The Architectural Impact of Violating SRP"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"When SRP is ignored, systems suffer from high coupling and low cohesion. Changes in business logic ripple across unrelated domains, leading to fragile architectures. Teams find it difficult to merge code because multiple developers are modifying the same monolithic classes for entirely different features."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Fat Models in Modern Frameworks"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"In many modern MVC frameworks, developers fall into the 'Fat Model' anti-pattern. Models are assigned database operations, validation logic, serialization rules, and notification dispatchers. This turns a simple data structure into an architectural bottleneck."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"class User {
    // Database logic
    public function save() { /* ... */ }

    // Business and notification logic
    public function sendWelcomeEmail() { /* ... */ }

    // Formatting logic
    public function toJson() { /* ... */ }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Anatomy of a God Class"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The ultimate evolution of an SRP violation is the 'God Class'. A God Class knows too much or does too much. It typically accumulates thousands of lines of code, manages multiple subsystems, and acts as a central hub for the entire application, making unit testing nearly impossible."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"High cyclomatic complexity with numerous conditional branches."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Extensive dependency lists requiring massive mock setups for tests."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Frequent merge conflicts due to team bottlenecks."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Refactoring Toward SRP"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"To resolve Fat Models and God Classes, extract specific responsibilities into dedicated service classes, repositories, and value objects. By separating persistence, domain logic, and presentation, you create a maintainable and testable architecture."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"class UserRegistrationService {
    private UserRepository $repository;
    private EmailNotifier $notifier;

    public function register(UserDto $dto): void {
        $user = new User($dto);
        $this->repository->save($user);
        $this->notifier->sendWelcomeEmail($user);
    }
}"}]
